.TH URLENCODE 1
.SH NAME
urlencode \- URL percent-encode/decode filter
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B urlencode
[
.B -d
]
[
.I file
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Urlencode
is a real, structural translation of Plan 9's own
.IR urlencode (1)
(from
.BR sys/src/cmd/urlencode.c ,
9front only - see BUGS), compiled to Dis and run by
.IR emu ,
not a host binary. It lives under
.B p9/cmd
rather than
.BR appl/cmd ;
see
.B p9/dis/README
for how to bind it in.
.PP
With no
.B -d
,
.I urlencode
copies
.I file
(or standard input) to standard output, percent-encoding every byte
that is not a letter, digit, or one of
.BR "/$-_@.!*'()," ,
and turning a space into
.BR + .
With
.BR -d ,
it does the reverse: a
.B +
becomes a space, and a
.B %
followed by two hex digits becomes the byte those digits spell out.
.PP
A malformed
.B %
sequence during decode is handled leniently rather than treated as an
error: if the character right after
.B %
is not a hex digit, only the
.B %
itself is emitted literally and that character is reprocessed on its
own; if the SECOND character is not a hex digit, both the
.B %
and the (valid) first digit are emitted literally, and the second
character is reprocessed on its own.
.SH SOURCE
.B /p9/cmd/urlencode.b
(translated from
.BR sys/src/cmd/urlencode.c ;
see its own header comment for the full derivation, including a real
translation bug found and fixed, negative-control-confirmed)
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR urlencodetest (1)
.SH BUGS
No plan9port copy of this file exists, and no prebuilt binary for it
existed on this host either - unlike every other file in this
backlog's own oracle situation. Rather than translate unverified, the
real oracle was BUILT from the actual 9front source at test time (see
.IR urlencodetest (1)'s
own header comment) - a new precedent for this backlog: a file with no
prebuilt binary anywhere is not automatically unverifiable if the host
has a C toolchain that can build the actual source directly.
.PP
Operates on
.B "array of byte"
throughout, not Limbo
.BR string ,
since this is a byte-transparent filter - decoding can reconstruct any
byte 0-255, including ones that are not valid standalone UTF-8, and a
string round-trip could corrupt exactly the high-byte values this
command exists to handle faithfully.
